WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.024
Paper discrete graph

00:00:01.836 --> 00:00:03.168
diffusion models are

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:04.418
secretly memorizing

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.600
Adarsh Jamadandi and

00:00:06.424 --> 00:00:07.780
Nicolas Keriven show

00:00:07.591 --> 00:00:08.369
for the first

00:00:08.181 --> 00:00:09.230
time that discrete

00:00:09.042 --> 00:00:10.527
graph diffusion models

00:00:10.398 --> 00:00:11.883
memorize training graphs

00:00:12.108 --> 00:00:13.016
even though sample

00:00:12.827 --> 00:00:14.053
metrics like edge

00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:15.575
overlap and WL

00:00:15.386 --> 00:00:17.013
kernel similarity suggest

00:00:16.825 --> 00:00:17.520
they don't

00:00:18.046 --> 00:00:19.602
Discrete graph diffusion

00:00:19.414 --> 00:00:20.569
models add noise

00:00:20.381 --> 00:00:21.737
independently to nodes

00:00:21.548 --> 00:00:22.291
and edges

00:00:22.515 --> 00:00:24.130
an equivariant denoiser

00:00:23.942 --> 00:00:24.732
trained on a

00:00:24.544 --> 00:00:26.064
single graph appears

00:00:25.876 --> 00:00:26.996
to generate novel

00:00:26.808 --> 00:00:27.527
graphs

00:00:28.046 --> 00:00:29.484
By analyzing reverse

00:00:29.296 --> 00:00:30.675
diffusion trajectories

00:00:30.487 --> 00:00:31.701
of models trained

00:00:31.513 --> 00:00:33.057
on two-block stochastic

00:00:32.869 --> 00:00:34.142
block model graphs

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.416
the authors find

00:00:35.228 --> 00:00:36.595
memorization hiding

00:00:36.407 --> 00:00:37.185
underneath

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:38.724
Full story on

00:00:38.535 --> 00:00:39.431
the dashboard
